 ### [Ray Tracing](8334-ray-tracing.md)
-Classic ray tracing (Whitted-style) is a recursive rendering model that captures shadows, specular reflection, refraction, and diffuse surface shading by tracing light paths from the image plane into the scene. It differs from simple ray casting by explicitly spawning secondary rays for reflection and refraction, and shadow rays for occlusion testing, thereby supporting native recursive ray propagation.
+Classic ray tracing (Whitted-style) is a rendering model that captures shadows, specular reflection, refraction, and diffuse surface shading by placing an image plane in the scene and sending rays through each pixel. It entails ray casting with the additional tracing of secondary rays for shadows and refractions. It sets itself apart from basic intersection methods by explicitly managing recursive light paths and secondary ray generation, and is chosen when physically accurate global illumination effects are required.
 
 ### [Multi-Sphere Ray Tracing with Diffuse Lighting](11351-multi-sphere-ray-tracing-with-diffuse-lighting.md)
-This method casts a primary ray against a fixed set of spheres to find the closest intersection and computes unshadowed diffuse lighting from multiple point lights. It differs from [Ray Tracing](8334-ray-tracing.md) by omitting recursive shadow rays and reflections, and from [Binary Search IsoSurface Extraction](1316-binary-search-isosurface-extraction.md) by using explicit geometric primitives rather than implicit field functions.
+This method casts a primary ray against a fixed set of spheres to find the closest intersection and computes unshadowed diffuse lighting from multiple point lights. It omits recursive shadow rays and reflections, distinguishing it from full ray tracing implementations [G]. It sets itself apart by restricting the scene to explicit spheres and direct illumination only, and is chosen for lightweight, deterministic lighting in constrained environments.
 
 ### [Screen-Space Ray Tracing with Depth Buffer](13547-screen-space-ray-tracing-with-depth-buffer.md)
 Screen-Space Ray Tracing with Depth Buffer (SSRT) is a technique to trace rays in screen space against a depth buffer to find surface intersections, supporting up to four depth layers per pixel. It solves the problem of efficiently determining ray-surface intersections for effects like reflections, refractions, and ambient occlusion without traversing complex scene graphs or voxel structures. What sets it apart from [Binary Search IsoSurface Extraction](1316-binary-search-isosurface-extraction.md) and [Trilinear Isosurface Intersection](2213-trilinear-isosurface-intersection.md) is that it operates directly on the rendered depth buffer rather than a volumetric or implicit representation, allowing it to capture any geometry already present in the frame buffer. Unlike [Ray Tracing](8334-ray-tracing.md) which traverses acceleration structures, SSRT traverses the 2D screen grid, making it highly parallelizable on GPUs but limited to visible surfaces.
 
+### [Parabolic Ray Tracing in Heterogeneous Media](14551-parabolic-ray-tracing-in-heterogeneous-media.md)
+Parabolic Ray Tracing is a method for computing light ray trajectories in media with spatially varying Refractive Index Fields (RIF) by approximating the RIF such that ray paths are second-order polynomials within tetrahedral cells. It solves the problem of exact intersection computation with cell boundaries, which is difficult when ray trajectories involve transcendental functions, by reducing the intersection problem to solving quadratic equations. It differs from linear ray tracing methods by accounting for continuous curvature within cells, and from general non-linear solvers by providing closed-form analytical solutions for the trajectory.
+
+### [Surface Area Heuristic (SAH)](14562-surface-area-heuristic-sah.md)
+The Surface Area Heuristic (SAH) is a cost model used to determine the optimal partitioning of primitives in Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) construction, both for top-down recursive splitting and iterative insertion. It minimizes the estimated ray tracing cost by leveraging the probability that a ray intersects a bounding volume, which is proportional to its surface area. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Binary Search IsoSurface Extraction](1316-binary-search-isosurface-extraction.md) or [Trilinear Isosurface Intersection](2213-trilinear-isosurface-intersection.md) by operating on the acceleration structure's topology rather than the geometric intersection logic itself, providing a general framework for spatial partitioning efficiency.
